@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Pager}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Pager @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Pager @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>
<p>
    The structure of the component is simply a <span class="code-span">nav</span> element that contains either buttons or links dependant on the components configuration options.
    The  <span class="code-span">nav</span> element has the <span class="code-span">aria-labelledby</span> attribute that uses two identifiers. The first part is
    the <span class="code-span">AriaLabel</span> parameter value, such as Data Pager,  the second points to the page information seen above the buttons or links, so screen reader
    users on entering the pager or via landmark lists, will have both the name of the pager and its state announced such as Data Pager, Current page 5 of 10.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>The two identifier approach is only used for Blazor WASM, when its Blazor Server only the first identifier is used and the live region service is then utilised
    to announce the page information. It was architected this way to negate certain issues that JAWS has with the Blazor Server diff mechanism and the way in which it handles its virtual buffers.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Important: </strong>Given the current JAWS virtual cursor bug as listed below, you should consider whether or not to set the <span class="code-span">AddApplicationRole</span> parameter to true,
    or just to add text to inform JAWS users to turn of the virtual cursor whilst on the pager selectors. Enabling this option disables the arrow keys for all screen readers, whilst on the
    pager selectors. It is highly unlikely that this would be an issue for the user as if they needed to read the button text they would just turn on the virtual cursor, and or tab away and back again
    and it would be announced as usual.
</p>

<h2 id="pager-keyboard">Keyboard interaction</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="pager-keyboard">
    <li><kbd>Tab, Shift + Tab</kbd> - to set focus on a link or button, and move in either direction.</li>
    <li><kbd>Enter or Space</kbd> - when on a button activates it and changes the data page.</li>
    <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> - when on a link activates it and changes result page.</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>These keyboard interactions are primarily aimed at keyboard-only users. Screen reader users will often navigate differently using their assistive technology's
    built-in commands, though the patterns also apply when a screen reader is operating in interaction mode.
</p>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>
<h2 id="screen-reader-quirks">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li>
        <b>Narrator - </b>The Pager uses the <span class="code-span">aria-disabled</span> attribute when buttons or links are not needed such as First and Previous when on the first page.
        If an <span class="code-span">aria-disabled</span> item is activated the current pager information is just re-announced, however, Narrator blocks this interaction
        when <span class="code-span">aria-disabled</span> is present.
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <strong>JAWS Virtual Cursor Bug</strong> There appears to be a virtual cursor bug that affects both Server and WASM when using the newer combined
            Blazor Web App Template choice - Auto (Server and WebAssembly). Using Server (Global) or WASM (Global) seem unaffected by the description below.
        </p>
        <p>
            When you repeatedly press a pager button such as Next, JAWS will lose/move focus. As a workaround an optional <span class="code-span">AddApplicationRole</span> parameter
            was added to the Pager component that when set to true adds the attribute and value <span class="code-span">role="application"</span> to the element that contains the pager
            buttons or links. This effectively disables the Virtual cursor whilst on the pager selectors. The alternative whilst the JAWS team resolve the issue is to advise JAWS users to
            turn off the virtual cursor whilst using the pager selectors.
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Note: </b>This site uses the older Standalone WASM template which appears to be unaffected by the aforementioned JAWS issue and as such the role attribute was not added to the pager tests.
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
